($ml$c3cff0b8-ff61-5fdb-8ccc-836778e94909$ml$::uuid, $ml$d4466bd8-9f6e-531f-bbf3-dcc1e50d01a7$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 23 · The rule$ml$, $ml$In 2008, just 9% of US hospitals had a basic electronic health record; by 2024, over 99% of non-federal acute care hospitals used a certified EHR. The shift followed the 2009 HITECH Act, which created the federal Health IT Certification Program. Office-based physicians went from 17% adoption in 2008 to 91% in 2024. Certification sets the standards EHR software must meet.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.healthit.gov/data/quickstats/national-trends-hospital-and-physician-adoption-electronic-health-records", "label": "ONC, national trends in EHR adoption"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$854f6130-1659-5acd-90b6-f084ad5bd191$ml$::uuid, $ml$d4466bd8-9f6e-531f-bbf3-dcc1e50d01a7$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The law$ml$, $ml$The HITECH Act of 2009 pushed providers to adopt electronic records. It created the Health IT Certification Program.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.healthit.gov/data/quickstats/national-trends-hospital-and-physician-adoption-electronic-health-records", "label": "ONC, national trends in EHR adoption"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$19337979-306b-5820-9f43-305a300d0622$ml$::uuid, $ml$d4466bd8-9f6e-531f-bbf3-dcc1e50d01a7$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$Certification$ml$, $ml$Certified EHRs meet federal standards for features and data exchange. The first products were certified in late 2010.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.healthit.gov/data/quickstats/national-trends-hospital-and-physician-adoption-electronic-health-records", "label": "ONC, national trends in EHR adoption"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$eae762bd-12c3-5859-b38f-a369804da00c$ml$::uuid, $ml$d4466bd8-9f6e-531f-bbf3-dcc1e50d01a7$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$17% to 91%$ml$, $ml$Office-based physicians adopting certified EHRs rose from 17% in 2008 to 91% in 2024.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.healthit.gov/data/quickstats/national-trends-hospital-and-physician-adoption-electronic-health-records", "label": "ONC, national trends in EHR adoption"}]$ml$::jsonb),
